WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.766 --> 00:00:06.005
♪ ♪

00:00:06.005 --> 00:00:12.078
♪ ♪

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:15.281
MICHAEL: Aw, this thing's heavy!
I need some dogs!

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:16.616
I have a new freight sled

00:00:16.616 --> 00:00:19.152
that I've never tested
on an overnight trip.

00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:22.388
So I'm gonna hook up
a small team, a four-dog team,

00:00:22.388 --> 00:00:26.826
and run out to Little Donna Lake
on an overnight camping trip.

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:28.761
It's the trail
that I've never been on.

00:00:28.761 --> 00:00:30.764
Usually when dogs
are running new territory,

00:00:30.764 --> 00:00:32.898
they're kind of anxious
and nervous.

00:00:32.898 --> 00:00:35.335
So this trip is about
teaching them patience,

00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:36.869
testing all the equipment,

00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:41.474
and pushing them
with a back-to-back run.

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:44.343
(dogs barking)

00:00:44.343 --> 00:00:45.778
You know, this early season,

00:00:45.778 --> 00:00:47.647
usually you're pretty safe
on a lake.

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:52.117
But I don't know if we'll cross
some swamps or creeks.

00:00:52.117 --> 00:00:55.087
Being on a trail
that I've never been on,

00:00:55.087 --> 00:00:57.390
it just involves
a little bit more stress,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:01.927
a little bit more danger,
a little bit more risk.

00:01:01.927 --> 00:01:05.331
You don't know where you might
break through the ice.

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:08.901
♪ ♪

00:01:08.901 --> 00:01:13.506
♪ ♪

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:17.444
♪ ♪

00:01:17.444 --> 00:01:18.945
(dogs barking)

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:22.715
(howling)

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:24.617
All right, Mao's excited!

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:25.952
Lead dog Mao!

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:27.487
Are you ready to go?

00:01:29.788 --> 00:01:32.392
Big bully woolly!

00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:33.259
Come on, up-up!

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:34.760
Up-up! Paws!

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.629
I'll help you up, buddy.

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:39.131
It's important to mile up
these Alaskan malamutes.

00:01:39.131 --> 00:01:40.700
You know, these guys
are my athletes.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.168
They're my workforce.

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:44.403
I have an upcoming
expedition planned,

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:47.506
and I have an upcoming
caribou hunt planned as well.

00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:50.576
And that will require
a substantial amount of dogs,

00:01:50.576 --> 00:01:52.412
which you gotta
take things in pieces.

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:53.979
You just can't go all out.

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:54.847
Come on.

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:55.748
This is a transition.

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:58.284
You gotta crawl.
You gotta walk.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:01:59.318
Then you gotta run.

00:01:59.318 --> 00:02:01.521
And this is just the walk phase.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:05.024
These dogs are as excited
to get on the trail as I am.

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:07.827
♪ ♪

00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:08.995
All right!

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:11.097
Time to get out of here.

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:13.933
And hopefully have a good run
with no issues.

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:20.940
♪ ♪

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:26.345
JESSI: As it get's colder,
it's a lot more work out here.

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:29.849
It's a little harder
than I expected.

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:31.550
You have to work for everything
you get out here.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:36.555
♪ ♪

00:02:36.555 --> 00:02:41.561
♪ ♪

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:46.298
So, I got the pick,
the shovel, and the crates.

00:02:46.298 --> 00:02:48.868
Time to go!

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:50.069
Water's a resource out here,

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:51.937
just like our wood
and our food is.

00:02:51.937 --> 00:02:56.275
So, we're constantly having
to battle to get more water.

00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:58.310
Even though the river's
right next to the house, uh,

00:02:58.310 --> 00:02:59.578
it's still kind of a chore,

00:02:59.578 --> 00:03:01.747
and Chris clears all the snow
with the shovel,

00:03:01.747 --> 00:03:03.382
and then he's gotta use the axe

00:03:03.382 --> 00:03:06.586
to chip just the clear ice
away from the river.

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:08.454
This is, hands down,
the best way

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:10.356
to get drinking water
for the house.

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:13.793
♪ ♪

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:17.630
CHRIS: I am going to go down
to start shoveling.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.399
Wow, that's slick!

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:21.834
Every day we feel like
we're dehydrated

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:23.569
and we're fighting
to keep up with water.

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:26.472
If you don't have enough water,
you're gonna die.

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:29.008
Here's one piece of clear ice.
Look at that.

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:31.144
This clear ice, once you
melt it down and filter it out,

00:03:31.144 --> 00:03:32.344
this is some of
the best-tasting water

00:03:32.344 --> 00:03:33.979
that I've ever had out here.

00:03:33.979 --> 00:03:37.717
♪ ♪

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:39.752
This ice is opaque.

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:41.053
I want that clear stuff.

00:03:41.053 --> 00:03:42.688
I'm just after drinking water.

00:03:42.688 --> 00:03:46.359
As you can see here,
this ice is really clear.

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:48.828
But as we start to get
down farther to the river,

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:50.162
it starts to darken.

00:03:50.162 --> 00:03:53.065
And the darker it is, you know,
the more swamp is in it,

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:54.300
the more grass is in it.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.302
It even dyes your urine
different colors.

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:59.772
So from here to here,
this like two-foot section

00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:02.274
is where we come and harvest
our drinking ice.

00:04:02.274 --> 00:04:07.279
♪ ♪

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:12.284
♪ ♪

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:16.889
♪ ♪

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:19.057
JESSI: We've kind of got it
down pat.

00:04:19.057 --> 00:04:20.526
He gets some really big chunks,

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:23.029
and we bring that into the house
and have to break that down.

00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:26.432
But he also gets smaller chunks
that he hucks up the bank

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.901
and hopefully
into my waiting arms,

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.436
to put into the crate.

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:33.339
♪ ♪

00:04:33.339 --> 00:04:35.141
CHRIS: How full are we?

00:04:35.141 --> 00:04:36.943
JESSI: I'd say that's good.
CHRIS: Okay.

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:38.110
JESSI: We've got
all these big pieces,

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:40.346
plus these three crates
are all filled.

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:43.048
CHRIS: Nice!

00:04:43.048 --> 00:04:45.851
JESSI: It's a good water haul.

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:48.388
CHRIS: Come back tomorrow
and keep moving forward.

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:54.527
♪ ♪

00:04:54.527 --> 00:04:56.162
BRENT:
You just can't survive here

00:04:56.162 --> 00:05:00.199
unless you're 150% into it
and willing to put

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:03.268
all the blood, sweat,
and tears it takes.

00:05:03.268 --> 00:05:04.636
You have to have that passion

00:05:04.636 --> 00:05:07.306
in order to be able to survive
and make it out here.

00:05:07.306 --> 00:05:11.410
♪ ♪

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:13.613
Hey! Marble!

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:14.846
Come on! Come here!

00:05:14.846 --> 00:05:16.448
Come here, you little butthead!

00:05:16.448 --> 00:05:17.750
Okay!

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:18.617
See?

00:05:18.617 --> 00:05:20.453
This is what the problem is.

00:05:20.453 --> 00:05:24.590
You just like to break out
all the time, don't you?

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:28.060
We're gonna solve
that problem right now.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.896
We're just back
from a good training run

00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:32.731
and Ida's in town taking care
of some business there.

00:05:32.731 --> 00:05:33.966
So I've got some time to work

00:05:33.966 --> 00:05:35.701
on a few projects
around the homestead here

00:05:35.701 --> 00:05:37.836
for the next couple of days.

00:05:37.836 --> 00:05:39.738
Today's biggest and
most important project

00:05:39.738 --> 00:05:42.541
is fixing the puppy pen.

00:05:42.541 --> 00:05:43.876
We've got nine puppies.

00:05:43.876 --> 00:05:46.044
They're all around
three months old.

00:05:46.044 --> 00:05:47.413
Now, because of all the snow,

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:50.182
their pen's fence
has gotten really low.

00:05:50.182 --> 00:05:51.650
And so, they can jump right over

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.621
and they're jumping out
and causing problems.

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:58.657
This is the real problem area,
this section right here.

00:05:58.657 --> 00:06:02.027
We're gonna need to, uh,
build that fence up higher

00:06:02.027 --> 00:06:05.331
and then dig that last section
all the way down to the ground.

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:06.933
So, yeah, this is
super important

00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:09.301
and we're gonna, we're gonna
get 'er done today.

00:06:09.301 --> 00:06:12.372
And then hopefully you guys will
stay in this pen, huh?

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:17.910
This is totally
making it up as I go,

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:24.883
but I'm gonna try and get this
rope woven through this fence.

00:06:24.883 --> 00:06:26.252
So, having this pen like this

00:06:26.252 --> 00:06:27.853
and having the dogs
be able to be loose

00:06:27.853 --> 00:06:30.923
when they're this age
is super important.

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:33.192
For their socialization
and for their happiness,

00:06:33.192 --> 00:06:35.061
they need to be free,
running around.

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:37.062
So, this is really important
because of that.

00:06:37.062 --> 00:06:38.697
We can't have them breaking out.

00:06:38.697 --> 00:06:45.237
♪ ♪

00:06:45.237 --> 00:06:46.138
(chuckles)

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:49.041
Didn't say it
was gonna be pretty.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:51.210
The lifestyle here livin' on
at the homestead,

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:54.046
it's a life full of work.

00:06:55.781 --> 00:06:58.317
Out here, the boss
is Mother Nature.

00:06:58.317 --> 00:07:00.686
The boss is the homestead
and the dogs.

00:07:00.686 --> 00:07:02.954
And they're dictating
what I've gotta do every day

00:07:02.954 --> 00:07:05.591
and what's a higher priority.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:06.992
But that freedom to be able
to live out here

00:07:06.992 --> 00:07:08.594
with my 50 and 60 dogs

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:12.932
is well worth all the chores
and the extra work.

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:14.266
For me, it's my paradise.

00:07:14.266 --> 00:07:16.735
I wouldn't trade this lifestyle
for anything.

00:07:16.735 --> 00:07:19.705
♪ ♪

00:07:19.705 --> 00:07:22.308
Our snow level was here,
and the fence was here.

00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:25.043
So, we only had a couple feet,
now we've almost eight,

00:07:25.043 --> 00:07:28.147
nine feet of, uh, clearance
on the fence.

00:07:28.147 --> 00:07:31.217
And I think it's gonna keep
these puppies from breaking out.

00:07:31.217 --> 00:07:32.718
Huh, guys?

00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:35.354
Time to move on
to the next chore tomorrow.

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:37.722
Get some snow shoveled
off the roofs.

00:07:37.722 --> 00:07:39.191
But I'm hopefully gonna wake up

00:07:39.191 --> 00:07:41.727
to puppies in the pen
tomorrow morning.

00:07:41.727 --> 00:07:44.897
♪ ♪

00:07:44.897 --> 00:07:46.565
ALEX: I don't think
in my mind, like,

00:07:46.565 --> 00:07:48.601
"Oh, this will be,
this will be easy."

00:07:51.336 --> 00:07:52.738
It's better to just, you know,

00:07:52.738 --> 00:07:55.574
be open to whatever's
gonna happen.

00:07:55.574 --> 00:08:01.146
♪ ♪

00:08:01.146 --> 00:08:04.650
♪ ♪

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.785
Sipsy. You ready?

00:08:06.785 --> 00:08:08.621
Today, I'm really
thinking ahead,

00:08:08.621 --> 00:08:10.823
I'm thinking about springtime
when breakup comes.

00:08:14.259 --> 00:08:16.495
♪ ♪

00:08:16.495 --> 00:08:19.465
 NARRATOR: For Alex Javor,
 staying ahead of the season

00:08:19.465 --> 00:08:22.034
 is key to surviving
 his lifestyle.

00:08:22.034 --> 00:08:25.437
 With rising river waters
 and spring on the horizon,

00:08:25.437 --> 00:08:27.740
 he'll need to replace
 an old shift bridge

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:31.110
 in order to keep an escape path
 to civilization.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:35.948
♪ ♪

00:08:35.948 --> 00:08:41.420
♪ ♪

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:44.089
ALEX: So, when the creek runs,
like a small river,

00:08:44.089 --> 00:08:45.557
it's got
a really strong current.

00:08:45.557 --> 00:08:48.394
You know, you'll see big trees
blasting down through the creek.

00:08:48.394 --> 00:08:50.229
It's, uh, kind of
a serious force of nature

00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:51.997
you gotta contend with.

00:08:51.997 --> 00:08:55.467
If I got hurt or I need
to move fast,

00:08:55.467 --> 00:08:57.403
the bridge is just a, uh,
a lifeline

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:59.572
if you have an emergency.

00:08:59.572 --> 00:09:01.640
How do I get across this thing
without freezing,

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:02.975
without getting
all my stuff wet?

00:09:02.975 --> 00:09:03.976
You know, you can do it,

00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:05.911
but it's a big thing
you gotta deal with.

00:09:05.911 --> 00:09:07.246
You know, if you had
a bad situation,

00:09:07.246 --> 00:09:09.681
it could turn things
into a much worse situation.

00:09:09.681 --> 00:09:15.687
♪ ♪

00:09:15.687 --> 00:09:21.693
♪ ♪

00:09:21.693 --> 00:09:27.166
♪ ♪

00:09:27.166 --> 00:09:28.333
(groans)

00:09:28.333 --> 00:09:32.137
♪ ♪

00:09:32.137 --> 00:09:35.240
Uh, right where I'm standing,

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:38.310
the old bridge
used to go across here.

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:39.978
I think this is part,

00:09:39.978 --> 00:09:42.080
I think this is from
the original bridge.

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.650
There was two big trees
that went across

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:48.321
and then a bunch of planks
that spanned them.

00:09:48.321 --> 00:09:52.457
It washed away some point
after I had left here.

00:09:52.457 --> 00:09:56.395
Uh, you can see
this is what's happened.

00:09:56.395 --> 00:09:58.297
♪ ♪

00:10:05.271 --> 00:10:07.539
ALEX: It's not water,
it's, uh, overflow ice

00:10:07.539 --> 00:10:10.109
from when the creek ran.

00:10:10.109 --> 00:10:13.545
Puttin' the bridge here because,
basically, this is possible.

00:10:13.545 --> 00:10:15.714
It's tall enough
it won't flood.

00:10:15.714 --> 00:10:18.417
And then we got the bank over
here with lots of big trees.

00:10:18.417 --> 00:10:20.118
So, what I got in mind
is dead simple,

00:10:20.118 --> 00:10:23.289
just make the tree fall across.

00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:24.590
And that's it.

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:27.025
If you have trouble balancing,
you can kind of sit and scoot,

00:10:27.025 --> 00:10:29.361
or we could put up a rope,
you know,

00:10:29.361 --> 00:10:31.296
to help, to handhold onto.

00:10:31.296 --> 00:10:33.532
So, I'm just kind of, uh,
check out my trees here

00:10:33.532 --> 00:10:36.968
and try to figure out
which one, uh, would be best.

00:10:36.968 --> 00:10:39.337
I think this one might be good.

00:10:39.337 --> 00:10:43.342
The only good candidate
is this big one.

00:10:43.342 --> 00:10:46.878
It'll definitely go across,
all the way.

00:10:46.878 --> 00:10:49.382
I think at least one
I'll need to cut down.

00:10:49.382 --> 00:10:51.116
If the tree breaks
the wrong way,

00:10:51.116 --> 00:10:53.752
I want to kind of have
a clear path.

00:10:53.752 --> 00:10:54.987
That's it.

00:10:54.987 --> 00:10:57.823
Got my plan, so uh, you know,
it's just time to get to work.

00:10:57.823 --> 00:10:59.458
Make my bridge.

00:10:59.458 --> 00:11:01.226
Nothing to it but to do it.

00:11:01.226 --> 00:11:05.063
♪ ♪

00:11:05.063 --> 00:11:09.167
♪ ♪

00:11:09.167 --> 00:11:11.637
MICHAEL:
I worry about bad snowdrifts

00:11:11.637 --> 00:11:14.439
that set up like concrete.

00:11:14.439 --> 00:11:16.375
And threaten to throw
you and your sled

00:11:16.375 --> 00:11:19.010
off into no-man's-land.

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:22.781
There's a lot of anxiety
built up with a trip like this.

00:11:22.781 --> 00:11:26.018
♪ ♪

00:11:26.018 --> 00:11:31.557
♪ ♪

00:11:31.557 --> 00:11:32.858
(dogs howling)

00:11:32.858 --> 00:11:34.593
Calm down!

00:11:37.529 --> 00:11:38.763
All right!

00:11:38.763 --> 00:11:40.899
Gonna have a night ride here.

00:11:40.899 --> 00:11:42.701
This ought to be interesting.

00:11:42.701 --> 00:11:44.369
 NARRATOR:
 After traveling 60 miles

00:11:44.369 --> 00:11:47.506
 for a training run
 and overnight camping trip,

00:11:47.506 --> 00:11:49.474
 Michael Manzo
 is preparing his dogs

00:11:49.474 --> 00:11:52.678
 for a challenging journey
 to Little Donna Lake.

00:11:52.678 --> 00:11:55.013
 With no time to waste,
 he must traverse

00:11:55.013 --> 00:11:59.985
 this uncharted trail
 in complete darkness.

00:11:59.985 --> 00:12:02.854
MICHAEL: You know people,
because of climate change,

00:12:02.854 --> 00:12:05.324
are breaking through the ice
at times

00:12:05.324 --> 00:12:08.594
where they're normally used to
traveling and subsisting.

00:12:08.594 --> 00:12:10.863
Last year, there was
a tragic loss

00:12:10.863 --> 00:12:13.665
of about three lives
in one event.

00:12:13.665 --> 00:12:15.601
So out there on the trails,

00:12:15.601 --> 00:12:17.669
you have to keep your head
in the game.

00:12:17.669 --> 00:12:19.738
♪ ♪

00:12:19.738 --> 00:12:23.542
Hooking up on a new trail for
the first time, it's stressful.

00:12:23.542 --> 00:12:25.377
But you gotta get
out of your comfort zone

00:12:25.377 --> 00:12:28.113
to refine and get better.

00:12:28.113 --> 00:12:31.883
All right, this is
the last dog to hook up.

00:12:31.883 --> 00:12:34.219
Time to hit that open trail.

00:12:34.219 --> 00:12:36.255
Hyah, hyah, hyah, hyah!

00:12:36.255 --> 00:12:38.957
Here we go! Here we go!

00:12:38.957 --> 00:12:41.827
Holy smokes, you guys
are full of it today!

00:12:41.827 --> 00:12:44.897
Absolutely full of it!

00:12:44.897 --> 00:12:45.764
All right!

00:12:45.764 --> 00:12:47.766
Sled's handling good.

00:12:47.766 --> 00:12:50.769
The trail's a little bumpy
and inconsistent.

00:12:50.769 --> 00:12:54.038
But I'm actually able to get
this thing sitting upright.

00:12:54.038 --> 00:12:56.041
The dogs are really hoofing it.

00:12:56.041 --> 00:12:59.011
They look good.

00:12:59.011 --> 00:13:01.447
I really love being
out on the trail.

00:13:01.447 --> 00:13:02.948
It lifts my spirit.

00:13:02.948 --> 00:13:05.250
The dogs can sense
that I'm happy.

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:08.120
Good turn! Good boys!

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:09.955
I would not be able
to live like this

00:13:09.955 --> 00:13:13.292
if I was working that typical
dead-end job in the city.

00:13:13.292 --> 00:13:16.595
It takes years to learn a lot
of this stuff on your own:

00:13:16.595 --> 00:13:18.264
How to build dog sleds.

00:13:18.264 --> 00:13:19.798
How to tie up lines.

00:13:19.798 --> 00:13:20.966
How to live off the land.

00:13:20.966 --> 00:13:23.569
And you know, when you're
working for the man,

00:13:23.569 --> 00:13:27.506
you barely have enough time
to scratch your own butt.

00:13:27.506 --> 00:13:30.609
We're taking this trail
with steam.

00:13:30.609 --> 00:13:32.411
Whoa!

00:13:32.411 --> 00:13:34.513
Good boys.

00:13:34.513 --> 00:13:35.681
All right.

00:13:35.681 --> 00:13:36.882
I made it five miles.

00:13:36.882 --> 00:13:39.217
I'm at Donna Lake,
the dogs did great.

00:13:39.217 --> 00:13:40.419
Sled handled well.

00:13:40.419 --> 00:13:43.221
Everybody got here
safe and sound.

00:13:43.221 --> 00:13:46.124
It's time to give some water,

00:13:46.124 --> 00:13:48.660
and then get a meal
into these dogs.

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:52.264
They've never been
on this trail before.

00:13:52.264 --> 00:13:54.900
Now it's time to set up my camp
and get some rest.

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:56.535
It's been a hard run.

00:13:56.535 --> 00:14:01.539
♪ ♪

00:14:01.539 --> 00:14:03.776
BRENT:
You can't predict the weather,

00:14:03.776 --> 00:14:08.213
force Mother Nature, so you're
just rolling with the punches.

00:14:08.213 --> 00:14:11.016
♪ ♪

00:14:11.016 --> 00:14:14.853
♪ ♪

00:14:14.853 --> 00:14:16.488
Hi, guys!

00:14:16.488 --> 00:14:17.322
All right.

00:14:17.322 --> 00:14:18.290
Well, now that these guys

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:22.227
are all locked in
the pen again, huh?

00:14:22.227 --> 00:14:26.464
Today's project is, uh,
getting up on this roof.

00:14:26.464 --> 00:14:30.068
This snow's been piling up
for a long time now.

00:14:30.068 --> 00:14:31.436
Ida's out of town.

00:14:31.436 --> 00:14:34.239
It's the perfect time
to just get this project done.

00:14:34.239 --> 00:14:38.777
This stuff right here is a bad
deal because it jams up here,

00:14:38.777 --> 00:14:41.513
and then when the building
warms up a little bit,

00:14:41.513 --> 00:14:46.051
it can leak, uh, into the cabin.

00:14:46.051 --> 00:14:50.755
Snow is like
a double-edged sword.

00:14:50.755 --> 00:14:52.257
We really want it, we love it,

00:14:52.257 --> 00:14:54.059
I'm smiling every time
it comes down.

00:14:54.059 --> 00:14:57.328
But then, after a certain point
of snow, you're kind of like,

00:14:57.328 --> 00:14:58.997
"Okay, it can slow down now,"

00:14:58.997 --> 00:15:03.268
because every time it snows,
it adds more chores to our list.

00:15:03.268 --> 00:15:05.437
Got most of this glacier
off here.

00:15:05.437 --> 00:15:08.073
I'm gonna grab
that ladder down there.

00:15:08.073 --> 00:15:10.508
Francis! No chewing on it!

00:15:10.508 --> 00:15:13.745
And put it on this roof,
and then climb up onto the peak.

00:15:13.745 --> 00:15:14.947
Francis!

00:15:14.947 --> 00:15:17.649
No! No chewing!

00:15:17.649 --> 00:15:19.184
I'm gonna take
your chew toy away.

00:15:22.087 --> 00:15:24.156
I've spent a lot of time
up high on roofs.

00:15:24.156 --> 00:15:26.058
I've only fallen
off a couple of times.

00:15:26.058 --> 00:15:29.461
But, uh, out here, safety
is like our number one thing.

00:15:29.461 --> 00:15:32.097
This is a higher roof than most
of them here at the property.

00:15:32.097 --> 00:15:33.265
It's also steeper.

00:15:33.265 --> 00:15:34.299
If you fall off the roof,

00:15:34.299 --> 00:15:37.468
it could be
a life-or-death situation.

00:15:37.468 --> 00:15:38.937
I just have to have a good hold.

00:15:38.937 --> 00:15:41.272
Because if I get up on that,
on the top of the ladder

00:15:41.272 --> 00:15:44.609
and it starts to slide,
I could slide off the roof.

00:15:44.609 --> 00:15:46.378
Like I almost did right there.

00:15:49.982 --> 00:15:52.417
That edge is a little closer
than I thought.

00:15:52.417 --> 00:15:57.022
♪ ♪

00:15:57.022 --> 00:15:58.824
Wow, look at that! Progress!

00:16:01.993 --> 00:16:03.995
I would much rather be
running dogs right now,

00:16:03.995 --> 00:16:05.898
but this is just one of
those necessities, you know?

00:16:05.898 --> 00:16:08.967
It's one of those hundreds
of chores that we have to do

00:16:08.967 --> 00:16:12.271
to keep the homestead
and keep everything maintained.

00:16:12.271 --> 00:16:13.404
'Cause if I don't do this,

00:16:13.404 --> 00:16:15.840
it's gonna take me more time
down the road.

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:19.644
♪ ♪

00:16:19.644 --> 00:16:21.880
All right, well, we wrapped
this project up here.

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:23.314
All the snow is off.

00:16:23.314 --> 00:16:25.683
Thousands of pounds
of snow off the roof.

00:16:25.683 --> 00:16:27.185
And we still got some daylight,

00:16:27.185 --> 00:16:32.657
so, one project down,
millions of projects to go.

00:16:32.657 --> 00:16:36.494
♪ ♪

00:16:36.494 --> 00:16:40.132
♪ ♪

00:16:40.132 --> 00:16:42.300
CHRIS: Getting accomplished
is what is really awesome

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:44.202
about being out here.

00:16:44.202 --> 00:16:46.704
So sometimes you just have to
forget the aches and pains

00:16:46.704 --> 00:16:47.973
and push through.

00:16:47.973 --> 00:16:51.643
♪ ♪

00:16:51.643 --> 00:16:54.145
♪ ♪

00:16:54.145 --> 00:17:00.252
♪ ♪

00:17:00.252 --> 00:17:02.821
CHRIS: I'd like to cut
right about here.

00:17:02.821 --> 00:17:06.124
Close to the bank,
close to the river.

00:17:06.124 --> 00:17:07.993
Then, we'll go get the pumps
and we'll drop 'em in the water

00:17:07.993 --> 00:17:09.994
and get some water
up to the house.

00:17:09.994 --> 00:17:13.165
(chainsaw starts)

00:17:16.535 --> 00:17:18.370
 NARRATOR: Living remotely,
 it's critical

00:17:18.370 --> 00:17:20.906
 for Chris and Jessi
 to secure water,

00:17:20.906 --> 00:17:24.476
 not only for drinking,
 but for cooking and cleaning.

00:17:24.476 --> 00:17:27.378
 Today they will install
 a water pump system

00:17:27.378 --> 00:17:29.480
 in the hopes of storing
 more bottled water

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:30.882
 for the months ahead.

00:17:30.882 --> 00:17:33.251
CHRIS: Uh, metal shovel?

00:17:33.251 --> 00:17:35.220
JESSI: You want the Pulaski
or the shovel?

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:36.321
CHRIS: Let me see
that Pulaski first.

00:17:36.321 --> 00:17:38.724
I think that's gonna do
some damage.

00:17:38.724 --> 00:17:41.627
JESSI: ♪ Hi-ho! Hi-ho! ♪

00:17:43.661 --> 00:17:46.631
Looks like that was made
for getting ice.

00:17:46.631 --> 00:17:48.367
CHRIS: It's getting wet
down here.

00:17:48.367 --> 00:17:50.535
I'm gonna start
chipping off the sides now.

00:17:50.535 --> 00:17:55.908
♪ ♪

00:17:55.908 --> 00:17:57.609
Water's one of the most
frustrating things

00:17:57.609 --> 00:17:58.410
to get out here.

00:17:58.410 --> 00:17:59.744
We need it every day.

00:17:59.744 --> 00:18:01.413
Chipping ice, that's great
for drinking water.

00:18:01.413 --> 00:18:05.483
But we can't do that for washing
laundry and doing dishes.

00:18:05.483 --> 00:18:07.219
That requires a lot of water,

00:18:07.219 --> 00:18:09.921
so we're always trying
to come up with new ways.

00:18:09.921 --> 00:18:11.923
So we got
our nice clear ice here.

00:18:11.923 --> 00:18:14.226
And this is the beginning
of the overflow.

00:18:14.226 --> 00:18:16.361
And you see it's not very clear
and it's very yellow.

00:18:16.361 --> 00:18:20.732
It's the kind of dirty water
we're trying to get away from.

00:18:20.732 --> 00:18:22.868
It's damp down there,
so we're gonna pop through.

00:18:25.237 --> 00:18:26.671
There it is. We got water.

00:18:26.671 --> 00:18:28.106
JESSI: Nice!

00:18:28.106 --> 00:18:28.907
CHRIS: And hopefully, it just
fills right up to the top.

00:18:28.907 --> 00:18:29.907
JESSI: Oh, okay.

00:18:29.907 --> 00:18:31.009
It's cracking! It's cracking!

00:18:31.009 --> 00:18:32.277
CHRIS: It's just 'cause
it's relieving pressure,

00:18:32.277 --> 00:18:33.511
it's no problem.

00:18:33.511 --> 00:18:35.747
JESSI: It's cracking on the edge
and I don't want to be on it!

00:18:35.747 --> 00:18:37.382
CHRIS: Perfect.
JESSI: Nope, nope! Nope, nope!

00:18:37.382 --> 00:18:39.184
CHRIS: You know, there's always
a danger involved

00:18:39.184 --> 00:18:40.285
when you're doing stuff.

00:18:40.285 --> 00:18:41.720
If you break through
the shelf ice

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:44.189
and there's water underneath it,
you're going in.

00:18:44.189 --> 00:18:45.958
Well, we got
a perfect hole, love.

00:18:45.958 --> 00:18:47.325
JESSI: I'm sorry, I didn't
want to be over there

00:18:47.325 --> 00:18:48.827
if it's cracking.

00:18:48.827 --> 00:18:50.262
CHRIS: Just trust
in your husband.

00:18:50.262 --> 00:18:51.263
(Jessi laughs)

00:18:51.263 --> 00:18:52.864
It's excellent,
we finally got water.

00:18:52.864 --> 00:18:54.099
Again, this isn't
drinking water.

00:18:54.099 --> 00:18:55.734
This is kind of like
our utility water.

00:18:55.734 --> 00:18:57.102
It's kind of murky
and a little dingy,

00:18:57.102 --> 00:18:59.437
but it does great for dishes
and, uh, clothes

00:18:59.437 --> 00:19:01.673
and bath houses
and all sorts of stuff.

00:19:01.673 --> 00:19:03.775
We're hoping to get at least
a month's worth of water,

00:19:03.775 --> 00:19:06.344
so getting a lot of water
like this up to the cabin

00:19:06.344 --> 00:19:07.645
is gonna be excellent.

00:19:07.645 --> 00:19:08.747
JESSI: All right, so you
just need to go get

00:19:08.747 --> 00:19:09.815
the pumps and the hose?

00:19:09.815 --> 00:19:11.349
CHRIS: All at once,
we're gonna bring out the hoses

00:19:11.349 --> 00:19:13.018
and the pumps
in a straight line.

00:19:13.018 --> 00:19:15.587
I'm gonna get the pump into here
and get the hoses all connected.

00:19:15.587 --> 00:19:16.688
JESSI: Before it freezes?

00:19:16.688 --> 00:19:19.023
CHRIS: And then we'll
start plugging 'em in, yeah.

00:19:19.023 --> 00:19:23.194
♪ ♪

00:19:23.194 --> 00:19:24.897
Never used the pumps before.

00:19:24.897 --> 00:19:26.564
Uh, they're kind of like
a sump pump.

00:19:26.564 --> 00:19:28.032
We used to use them
for showers and such,

00:19:28.032 --> 00:19:29.067
but, uh, we're gonna try and see

00:19:29.067 --> 00:19:31.503
if we can pump some water
up the bank.

00:19:31.503 --> 00:19:37.175
♪ ♪

00:19:37.175 --> 00:19:39.310
See if we can get some water,
there it goes!

00:19:39.310 --> 00:19:42.514
JESSI: Hey, that's clear water.

00:19:42.514 --> 00:19:43.548
How's it feel?

00:19:43.548 --> 00:19:44.716
Doesn't feel
like it's warming up?

00:19:44.716 --> 00:19:46.084
CHRIS:
Yeah, it seems slower now.

00:19:46.084 --> 00:19:47.819
I'm gonna go down
and check the end.

00:19:50.522 --> 00:19:51.490
JESSI: Huh.

00:19:51.490 --> 00:19:53.191
CHRIS: Is that any better?

00:19:53.191 --> 00:19:55.326
JESSI: Seemed like it was
a lot better in the beginning.

00:19:55.326 --> 00:19:56.461
CHRIS: It's probably
just getting ice

00:19:56.461 --> 00:19:57.462
sucked up or something.

00:19:57.462 --> 00:19:59.331
JESSI: It's like a third
of what it was.

00:19:59.331 --> 00:20:00.865
Now there's nothing.

00:20:00.865 --> 00:20:02.334
We got zero water!

00:20:06.071 --> 00:20:07.806
JESSI: We got zero water!

00:20:10.108 --> 00:20:11.843
CHRIS: Maybe we're just
too high up.

00:20:11.843 --> 00:20:13.578
JESSI: It worked well
right at the beginning.

00:20:13.578 --> 00:20:15.046
CHRIS: It must have froze.

00:20:15.046 --> 00:20:16.214
JESSI: The actual pump itself?

00:20:16.214 --> 00:20:17.582
CHRIS: No, it's, the pump
is still pumping,

00:20:17.582 --> 00:20:19.250
I bet the line somewhere.

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:21.653
It's going through the snow
and it's cold out.

00:20:21.653 --> 00:20:23.555
I'm gonna go down
and see if there's, uh,

00:20:23.555 --> 00:20:24.789
some air in the line
or something.

00:20:24.789 --> 00:20:29.027
I'm gonna see if I can get it
to pump harder down there.

00:20:29.027 --> 00:20:31.396
JESSI: There's got to be
a bit of ice in the line.

00:20:31.396 --> 00:20:32.631
'Cause nothing's coming out,

00:20:32.631 --> 00:20:35.166
and the pump's right next
to the end of the hose.

00:20:35.166 --> 00:20:37.836
You want to use that red hose
instead of the white hose?

00:20:37.836 --> 00:20:40.271
CHRIS: Yeah, toss it down.

00:20:40.271 --> 00:20:42.708
Damn! Always something.

00:20:47.312 --> 00:20:49.381
JESSI: One of these days,
we'll figure it out;

00:20:49.381 --> 00:20:51.916
what we need to get water.

00:20:51.916 --> 00:20:53.017
CHRIS: Hold that end.

00:20:53.017 --> 00:20:57.889
♪ ♪

00:20:57.889 --> 00:20:59.123
JESSI: Uh, I'm gonna
get up there.

00:20:59.123 --> 00:21:00.859
I'll need you to kind of
toss this to me.

00:21:00.859 --> 00:21:02.594
CHRIS: Okay. Hand it back.

00:21:07.933 --> 00:21:09.567
Once you get back up,
just plug me in.

00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:13.872
JESSI: And I'm plugging in!

00:21:13.872 --> 00:21:15.306
CHRIS: I see water going!

00:21:15.306 --> 00:21:17.208
JESSI: Yup, yup, yup!

00:21:17.208 --> 00:21:18.343
CHRIS: All right!

00:21:18.343 --> 00:21:19.244
How's it coming out?

00:21:19.244 --> 00:21:20.479
Pretty quick?

00:21:20.479 --> 00:21:22.614
JESSI: It's like when you turn
your tap water on,

00:21:22.614 --> 00:21:25.049
it's like, uh, one third.

00:21:25.049 --> 00:21:26.385
CHRIS: All right, I'm coming up!

00:21:28.887 --> 00:21:30.588
That's going pretty quick.

00:21:30.588 --> 00:21:32.223
JESSI: Yeah, I'm not sure
it's any faster

00:21:32.223 --> 00:21:33.925
than us just dipping
our buckets in,

00:21:33.925 --> 00:21:36.361
but my back is thankful.

00:21:36.361 --> 00:21:37.662
CHRIS: Yeah, I'm not
slipping and falling

00:21:37.662 --> 00:21:40.198
and pouring water
all over myself.

00:21:40.198 --> 00:21:41.466
JESSI: All right,
you wanna take that one?

00:21:41.466 --> 00:21:42.367
CHRIS: I'll swap you out.

00:21:42.367 --> 00:21:44.569
I'll just bring this
right over to you.

00:21:44.569 --> 00:21:47.139
I thought this pump was going to
be the answer to our problems.

00:21:47.139 --> 00:21:48.173
Doesn't seem to be.

00:21:48.173 --> 00:21:49.407
But it's still easier

00:21:49.407 --> 00:21:51.576
than hauling buckets
full of water up the hill.

00:21:51.576 --> 00:21:53.177
So, frustrating as it is,

00:21:53.177 --> 00:21:55.646
it seems to be the best solution
we have right now.

00:21:55.646 --> 00:21:56.815
This worked today,

00:21:56.815 --> 00:21:58.550
but I don't think it's gonna
work once it gets colder.

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:05.390
♪ ♪

00:22:05.390 --> 00:22:08.426
♪ ♪

00:22:08.426 --> 00:22:09.628
ALEX: If I don't want
to do something,

00:22:09.628 --> 00:22:11.329
I just, I'm not doing it.

00:22:11.329 --> 00:22:13.765
But if I do want to do
something, you know,

00:22:13.765 --> 00:22:15.534
I'm gonna, I'm gonna do it
as best as I can.

00:22:19.771 --> 00:22:23.541
(chopping)

00:22:23.541 --> 00:22:28.913
♪ ♪

00:22:28.913 --> 00:22:30.115
There we go.

00:22:34.285 --> 00:22:35.987
(chuckles)

00:22:35.987 --> 00:22:37.889
Well, there's one.

00:22:39.924 --> 00:22:42.393
Now, we got the big guy.

00:22:42.393 --> 00:22:44.529
It's gonna be a lot of chopping.

00:22:44.529 --> 00:22:47.198
 NARRATOR: For Alex, having
 a bridge across the river

00:22:47.198 --> 00:22:51.435
 will ensure an emergency escape
 route once the water rises.

00:22:51.435 --> 00:22:53.838
 With one piece
 of the bridge secured,

00:22:53.838 --> 00:22:56.908
 he'll need to carefully fall
 a main massive tree

00:22:56.908 --> 00:22:58.643
 in just the right place.

00:22:58.643 --> 00:23:01.847
ALEX: Yeah, I got my, uh,
my rope and pulley set up,

00:23:01.847 --> 00:23:05.484
with this, you know, extra
bit of tension on the tree,

00:23:05.484 --> 00:23:09.387
that's gonna help guide it
to fall this way to towards me,

00:23:09.387 --> 00:23:12.957
versus wanting to go
some other way.

00:23:12.957 --> 00:23:14.358
This is like
a super massive tree,

00:23:14.358 --> 00:23:16.661
so I gotta be really careful.

00:23:16.661 --> 00:23:18.630
These ropes all have
kind of a maximum amount

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:20.065
of weight they can hold.

00:23:20.065 --> 00:23:22.366
If I put a thousand pounds
of pressure on it,

00:23:22.366 --> 00:23:23.901
the rope will probably break,

00:23:23.901 --> 00:23:29.807
and people do sometimes
get killed, um, from snap-back.

00:23:29.807 --> 00:23:31.709
Time to, time to get to work.

00:23:31.709 --> 00:23:36.547
♪ ♪

00:23:36.547 --> 00:23:39.918
♪ ♪

00:23:39.918 --> 00:23:43.254
This will be the biggest tree
I've ever cut down.

00:23:43.254 --> 00:23:45.557
That doesn't really
make me excited.

00:23:45.557 --> 00:23:47.091
To be honest,
it kind of makes me feel

00:23:47.091 --> 00:23:51.562
like I'm gonna feel relief
once it's over.

00:23:51.562 --> 00:23:53.464
You know, I like
pushing the limits,

00:23:53.464 --> 00:23:57.935
but not really
with stuff like this.

00:23:57.935 --> 00:24:01.539
It's like, uh, teasing forces
that could easily crush me.

00:24:01.539 --> 00:24:05.443
♪ ♪

00:24:05.443 --> 00:24:11.283
♪ ♪

00:24:11.283 --> 00:24:13.752
It's making noises!

00:24:13.752 --> 00:24:18.189
(cracking)

00:24:18.189 --> 00:24:20.791
♪ ♪

00:24:20.791 --> 00:24:24.396
Wow, now, that was something.

00:24:24.396 --> 00:24:25.830
Pretty awesome to witness.

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:27.665
But it did do
what I didn't want it to do,

00:24:27.665 --> 00:24:29.000
it popped down a bit.

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:30.802
And, uh, once
the creek water comes,

00:24:30.802 --> 00:24:32.904
it would probably grab it
and take it away.

00:24:32.904 --> 00:24:37.108
So I want to, I want to pull it
back up on shore a bit.

00:24:37.108 --> 00:24:38.409
First thing tomorrow,

00:24:38.409 --> 00:24:41.379
I'll come back out here
and finish this out strong.

00:24:41.379 --> 00:24:45.216
♪ ♪

00:24:45.216 --> 00:24:48.720
♪ ♪

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:49.954
MICHAEL:
There's a lot of unknowns

00:24:49.954 --> 00:24:52.957
when you're in new territory.

00:24:52.957 --> 00:24:54.559
And you can never guarantee

00:24:54.559 --> 00:24:59.030
that you're gonna
make it home safely.

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:02.066
That's what I struggle with,
is the anxiety of the unknown.

00:25:02.066 --> 00:25:04.969
♪ ♪

00:25:04.969 --> 00:25:06.871
All right, camp is broke down.

00:25:06.871 --> 00:25:10.141
You know, this is all about
a training run for the dogs,

00:25:10.141 --> 00:25:13.011
but if I could sneak in a little
bit of fishing, I'm a happy man.

00:25:13.011 --> 00:25:17.182
So, I'm gonna try my hand
at fishing this lake.

00:25:17.182 --> 00:25:21.553
Maybe I might catch
a char or a trout.

00:25:21.553 --> 00:25:26.157
So, this is just jigging,
and this is my ice-fishing pole.

00:25:26.157 --> 00:25:27.959
You don't really use
a long pole,

00:25:27.959 --> 00:25:29.861
usually, you can use a stick

00:25:29.861 --> 00:25:33.097
and you send a lure or some bait
down in the hole.

00:25:33.097 --> 00:25:35.199
And every so often,
you just move it a little bit.

00:25:35.199 --> 00:25:37.068
The fish are sluggish
in the winter.

00:25:37.068 --> 00:25:40.404
So, you gotta just wait it out
and take your time.

00:25:40.404 --> 00:25:43.174
Just like it takes patience
with the dogs,

00:25:43.174 --> 00:25:44.975
ice fishing takes patience, too.

00:25:44.975 --> 00:25:46.944
Sometimes you come away
with a bunch of fish,

00:25:46.944 --> 00:25:49.447
sometimes you don't
catch nothing.

00:25:49.447 --> 00:25:52.950
So, we had a great run-in
to this beautiful campsite,

00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:56.621
and now we got all this ice fog
sitting here.

00:25:56.621 --> 00:25:59.357
It's kind of
a majestic landscape.

00:25:59.357 --> 00:26:01.259
This is kind of like
my vacation time,

00:26:01.259 --> 00:26:03.861
and I honestly don't get
to fish much.

00:26:03.861 --> 00:26:05.996
You know, I'm busy
catching moose,

00:26:05.996 --> 00:26:07.898
busy catching winter caribou.

00:26:07.898 --> 00:26:09.801
I only probably get about
a couple of fishing trips

00:26:09.801 --> 00:26:11.102
in a year.

00:26:11.102 --> 00:26:17.275
And if I can get a fish,
that'd be a real nice treat.

00:26:17.275 --> 00:26:19.477
All right, I've been going at it
for a few minutes,

00:26:19.477 --> 00:26:21.845
haven't got any bites.

00:26:21.845 --> 00:26:23.381
But I'm running out of daylight.

00:26:23.381 --> 00:26:25.950
As much as I'd like to put
more focus on this lake,

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:29.254
drilling holes here and there,
trying to locate the fish...

00:26:29.254 --> 00:26:30.388
Ooh!

00:26:34.459 --> 00:26:36.761
MICHAEL: Ooh! There we go.

00:26:36.761 --> 00:26:39.263
Got a big one.

00:26:39.263 --> 00:26:40.832
Just as I was thinking
about packing up,

00:26:40.832 --> 00:26:43.168
I finally hit a fish.

00:26:43.168 --> 00:26:45.436
That's a beautiful trout.

00:26:45.436 --> 00:26:47.472
That'll go good
in the frying pan.

00:26:47.472 --> 00:26:50.641
It's such a tease, because
where there's one, there's more,

00:26:50.641 --> 00:26:54.012
but I'm about ready to leave
out of here and pack up,

00:26:54.012 --> 00:26:58.215
'cause the ice fog is coming in
and the visibility is reducing.

00:26:58.215 --> 00:27:00.451
I think there may be
a storm coming.

00:27:00.451 --> 00:27:03.421
So it's time to transition.

00:27:03.421 --> 00:27:06.991
This is a bonus of the trip,
catching a nice fish like that.

00:27:06.991 --> 00:27:10.261
But unfortunately, we gotta get
these dogs out of here

00:27:10.261 --> 00:27:12.297
before this storm comes in,

00:27:12.297 --> 00:27:14.899
get home safely and
call this a successful run.

00:27:14.899 --> 00:27:17.835
♪ ♪

00:27:17.835 --> 00:27:21.672
Whoo! Go get 'em! Go get 'em!

00:27:21.672 --> 00:27:24.576
Hyah-hyah-hyah!
There we go! There we go!

00:27:24.576 --> 00:27:27.278
The dogs, I've been running them
for a half a decade.

00:27:27.278 --> 00:27:29.480
And I get better
and the dogs get better

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:31.549
as the years progress.

00:27:31.549 --> 00:27:33.918
These malamutes,
they're not machines.

00:27:33.918 --> 00:27:35.520
They are living beings.

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:37.588
They have emotions
of excitement,

00:27:37.588 --> 00:27:39.290
emotions of nervousness.

00:27:39.290 --> 00:27:41.125
It's quite a challenge.

00:27:41.125 --> 00:27:43.861
And this run is
to really get dialed in

00:27:43.861 --> 00:27:47.332
so that come expedition time,
or come caribou hunt,

00:27:47.332 --> 00:27:48.767
we're ready for it.

00:27:48.767 --> 00:27:50.902
I would call this trip
a success.

00:27:50.902 --> 00:27:56.007
♪ ♪

00:27:56.007 --> 00:27:58.009
BRENT: My to-do list
only gets bigger.

00:27:58.009 --> 00:27:59.978
It doesn't shrink,
it never goes away.

00:27:59.978 --> 00:28:02.714
It'll never, you'll never
catch up. (laughs)

00:28:05.316 --> 00:28:10.454
♪ ♪

00:28:10.454 --> 00:28:13.224
Up the mountain we go.

00:28:13.224 --> 00:28:16.961
See how long my stuff
lasts in the sled.

00:28:16.961 --> 00:28:20.164
I just finished up getting
all the snow off the roof.

00:28:20.164 --> 00:28:23.601
So that's a big project
checked off the list.

00:28:23.601 --> 00:28:24.869
We still have some light
in the day,

00:28:24.869 --> 00:28:27.004
so we're gonna head up
to check out my comms.

00:28:27.004 --> 00:28:28.906
It's, we have
our satellite Internet.

00:28:28.906 --> 00:28:31.042
It frequently gets blown in

00:28:31.042 --> 00:28:33.144
and snow can get into the box
where the modem is,

00:28:33.144 --> 00:28:36.481
so we gotta check that out.

00:28:36.481 --> 00:28:38.850
We don't have a very
conventional Internet setup.

00:28:38.850 --> 00:28:40.618
We get the signal
from the satellite dish,

00:28:40.618 --> 00:28:42.253
it goes down into a cooler

00:28:42.253 --> 00:28:44.422
that's up there
where the modem sits.

00:28:44.422 --> 00:28:46.924
That sends the signal
to this bridge.

00:28:46.924 --> 00:28:49.027
From there, it goes
into this CAT 5 wire,

00:28:49.027 --> 00:28:53.298
that goes down to this PVC pipe
that goes down through the trees

00:28:53.298 --> 00:28:55.166
across the trail
and down to the shop,

00:28:55.166 --> 00:28:57.301
which is another 400 feet
down there.

00:28:57.301 --> 00:28:59.237
There's a lot
of bush engineering,

00:28:59.237 --> 00:29:01.305
is what we call it.

00:29:01.305 --> 00:29:03.074
So we're going up there
to check this system out

00:29:03.074 --> 00:29:05.242
to make sure the modem's
not buried in snow.

00:29:05.242 --> 00:29:06.277
Something we got to do it
all the time,

00:29:06.277 --> 00:29:08.379
'cause it's our only source
of communication

00:29:08.379 --> 00:29:09.914
with the real world.

00:29:12.616 --> 00:29:16.287
All right, well, right here you
can see the wind and the snow

00:29:16.287 --> 00:29:19.791
has totally buried
that Igloo cooler.

00:29:21.726 --> 00:29:23.361
Out here you have to play
all the roles,

00:29:23.361 --> 00:29:24.829
and here you gotta play
the cable guy.

00:29:24.829 --> 00:29:29.133
You know, you just can't call
somebody to come and fix this.

00:29:29.133 --> 00:29:30.768
Living remote
like we do out here,

00:29:30.768 --> 00:29:32.437
it's super important to have

00:29:32.437 --> 00:29:34.705
a really solid line
of communication.

00:29:34.705 --> 00:29:37.842
We're 150 miles
down a mostly gravel road

00:29:37.842 --> 00:29:40.010
that has very unpredictable
weather conditions

00:29:40.010 --> 00:29:42.279
throughout most of the year,
especially in the wintertime.

00:29:42.279 --> 00:29:45.449
And so our lifeline
is the satellite Internet.

00:29:45.449 --> 00:29:48.085
It connects us enough
so that if we get in trouble,

00:29:48.085 --> 00:29:50.521
we can reach out
and get that help.

00:29:50.521 --> 00:29:51.589
Moment of truth here.

00:29:51.589 --> 00:29:54.925
We'll see what everything
looks like inside.

00:29:54.925 --> 00:29:59.497
Unbelievably, it looks
pretty darn good.

00:29:59.497 --> 00:30:03.167
It's pretty amazing,
this modem here is, uh, warm.

00:30:03.167 --> 00:30:05.203
And the cables are all warm,

00:30:05.203 --> 00:30:09.340
the outlet, everything
is warm and dry.

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:11.108
Everything is operational.

00:30:11.108 --> 00:30:13.177
Success.

00:30:13.177 --> 00:30:14.578
The check-up is good.

00:30:14.578 --> 00:30:16.981
Ida comes back tonight.

00:30:16.981 --> 00:30:20.117
Quick trip home, and have
a good day in the books.

00:30:20.117 --> 00:30:25.589
♪ ♪

00:30:25.589 --> 00:30:29.360
JESSI: I'm just
pushing myself more.

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:31.562
Because in the past,
I haven't pushed myself,

00:30:31.562 --> 00:30:34.132
and I haven't succeeded,
and it stinking sucks.

00:30:37.001 --> 00:30:42.373
♪ ♪

00:30:42.373 --> 00:30:43.907
CHRIS: All right!

00:30:43.907 --> 00:30:46.611
You ready to cut
some blocks of ice?

00:30:46.611 --> 00:30:48.813
Do you want to get
your small chainsaw

00:30:48.813 --> 00:30:51.883
and cut me a black spruce?

00:30:51.883 --> 00:30:54.718
 NARRATOR: After getting a small
 amount of water for drinking

00:30:54.718 --> 00:30:57.021
 and a failed attempt
 at a pump system,

00:30:57.021 --> 00:30:58.623
 Chris and Jessi
 are going to try

00:30:58.623 --> 00:31:02.827
 a traditional yet extremely
 dangerous way of getting water:

00:31:02.827 --> 00:31:06.864
 pulling large chunks of ice
 directly from a frozen river.

00:31:06.864 --> 00:31:09.200
CHRIS: My dad used to come out
to the river and harvest ice.

00:31:09.200 --> 00:31:11.936
I've never done it,
and I want to try and learn.

00:31:11.936 --> 00:31:13.837
Figure I take advantage
of the warm weather,

00:31:13.837 --> 00:31:15.840
add to our stockpile of water.

00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:18.009
We need it every day,
and it's hard to get.

00:31:18.009 --> 00:31:20.778
If it gets any colder out here,
then it's gonna be a lot harder.

00:31:20.778 --> 00:31:23.081
I need to get as much
as possible right now.

00:31:26.717 --> 00:31:28.452
This is not free yet.

00:31:28.452 --> 00:31:29.553
Oh, there it goes. Okay.

00:31:29.553 --> 00:31:30.421
JESSI: Nice!

00:31:30.421 --> 00:31:32.790
CHRIS: You got
that small chainsaw?

00:31:32.790 --> 00:31:35.193
(starts chainsaw)

00:31:35.193 --> 00:31:37.661
(buzzing)

00:31:37.661 --> 00:31:41.499
♪ ♪

00:31:41.499 --> 00:31:45.903
♪ ♪

00:31:45.903 --> 00:31:47.672
See if I can get this
through the hole.

00:31:49.840 --> 00:31:52.443
Okay. Something to poke it
through with.

00:31:57.882 --> 00:31:59.149
All right.

00:31:59.149 --> 00:32:02.486
I'm just gonna have to hit it
hard with the snow machine.

00:32:02.486 --> 00:32:05.056
Let's see what happens, huh?

00:32:05.056 --> 00:32:07.392
Hopefully,
we don't bust a stick.

00:32:07.392 --> 00:32:10.828
(engine starts)

00:32:10.828 --> 00:32:11.863
All right!

00:32:13.664 --> 00:32:15.433
JESSI: Whoa!
CHRIS: What happened?

00:32:15.433 --> 00:32:16.633
JESSI: It got pulled
and snapped.

00:32:16.633 --> 00:32:18.702
What do you think about me,
like, levering from the back?

00:32:18.702 --> 00:32:19.670
CHRIS: Yeah.

00:32:19.670 --> 00:32:21.072
JESSI: I just need
something that's bigger

00:32:21.072 --> 00:32:22.941
than this little stick
that I have.

00:32:26.076 --> 00:32:27.812
CHRIS: Wow, that's big!

00:32:27.812 --> 00:32:29.780
That thing probably
weighs 300 pounds.

00:32:29.780 --> 00:32:31.148
Hopefully we can get it out.

00:32:31.148 --> 00:32:33.050
That's a big chunk of ice
to bring home.

00:32:33.050 --> 00:32:34.751
I'm gonna pick up on this rope

00:32:34.751 --> 00:32:36.788
and I'm gonna have you
stick the fat end down.

00:32:36.788 --> 00:32:38.422
JESSI: Okay.
CHRIS: If possible.

00:32:38.422 --> 00:32:40.324
JESSI: I'm trying.
CHRIS: You're doing great.

00:32:40.324 --> 00:32:42.793
JESSI: I'm beginning to hate
getting water.

00:32:42.793 --> 00:32:44.528
CHRIS: What's that?
JESSI: Nothing!

00:32:44.528 --> 00:32:45.863
CHRIS: I forget
that Jessi doesn't have

00:32:45.863 --> 00:32:47.231
the strength that I have.

00:32:47.231 --> 00:32:48.565
Yeah, I thought this was
gonna be easier.

00:32:48.565 --> 00:32:49.667
JESSI: No kidding.

00:32:49.667 --> 00:32:50.701
CHRIS:
She's working really hard,

00:32:50.701 --> 00:32:51.802
she's doing the best she can.

00:32:51.802 --> 00:32:53.570
JESSI: I can't lever any more.

00:32:53.570 --> 00:32:54.672
CHRIS: Okay.

00:32:54.672 --> 00:32:56.474
We definitely work really well
as a team together.

00:32:56.474 --> 00:32:59.844
And as far as partners go,
she's kicking ass out here.

00:32:59.844 --> 00:33:01.745
JESSI: You got this, love,
don't worry.

00:33:01.745 --> 00:33:05.649
♪ ♪

00:33:05.649 --> 00:33:09.987
♪ ♪

00:33:09.987 --> 00:33:11.055
CHRIS: Holy cow!

00:33:11.055 --> 00:33:12.056
JESSI: Yeah.

00:33:12.056 --> 00:33:14.758
CHRIS: That is
a huge block of ice.

00:33:14.758 --> 00:33:16.560
JESSI: Oh, you're gonna
get soaked, my love.

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:19.229
CHRIS: Okay. I'm gonna try
to pick it up again.

00:33:19.229 --> 00:33:21.398
(struggling)

00:33:21.398 --> 00:33:23.200
Let's see if I can get it out.

00:33:23.200 --> 00:33:24.602
(grunts)

00:33:24.602 --> 00:33:25.969
JESSI: What are you
trying to do?

00:33:25.969 --> 00:33:27.304
Are you trying to get it
out of the hole?

00:33:27.304 --> 00:33:28.405
CHRIS: Yeah.

00:33:28.405 --> 00:33:30.007
JESSI: Just by your back power?

00:33:30.007 --> 00:33:31.542
CHRIS: Yeah, maybe just
stick the stick there

00:33:31.542 --> 00:33:32.676
and come help me.

00:33:32.676 --> 00:33:34.011
I can almost get it out.

00:33:34.011 --> 00:33:37.949
I just don't have enough oomph
to get it over the edge.

00:33:37.949 --> 00:33:40.317
One, two and up!

00:33:40.317 --> 00:33:41.652
♪ ♪

00:33:45.523 --> 00:33:47.558
CHRIS: Oh, don't let it,
you're holding me down!

00:33:47.558 --> 00:33:48.960
You're holding me down!

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:50.428
All this weight is on me.

00:33:50.428 --> 00:33:51.362
Move the rope!

00:33:51.362 --> 00:33:54.231
Let it go! Jeez!

00:33:54.231 --> 00:33:55.232
JESSI: All right.

00:33:55.232 --> 00:33:58.101
CHRIS: I am so soaked!

00:33:58.101 --> 00:34:00.571
Let's try to get it up and out.

00:34:00.571 --> 00:34:03.174
There it is!

00:34:03.174 --> 00:34:05.142
Holy (bleep)!

00:34:05.142 --> 00:34:07.144
I'm trying to get that
under the side.

00:34:07.144 --> 00:34:08.179
JESSI: Okay.

00:34:08.179 --> 00:34:09.947
CHRIS: I think this thing's
too big to come out.

00:34:09.947 --> 00:34:12.115
JESSI: I have a, I'm scared
of going in the hole.

00:34:12.115 --> 00:34:13.917
CHRIS: Don't go in the hole.
JESSI: Well...

00:34:13.917 --> 00:34:15.319
CHRIS: You won't come back up.

00:34:15.319 --> 00:34:16.553
Rock it.

00:34:16.553 --> 00:34:18.589
Okay.

00:34:18.589 --> 00:34:20.758
JESSI: When you pull it up,
it pulls right out of my hands.

00:34:20.758 --> 00:34:22.192
CHRIS: Then you pull it up.

00:34:22.192 --> 00:34:25.529
I just--You're not able
to pull it very hard.

00:34:25.529 --> 00:34:28.098
Okay.

00:34:28.098 --> 00:34:29.767
There it is.

00:34:29.767 --> 00:34:31.035
Go winch the winch!

00:34:31.035 --> 00:34:33.003
JESSI: Okay, got it?
CHRIS: Yeah.

00:34:33.003 --> 00:34:36.607
♪ ♪

00:34:36.607 --> 00:34:39.710
You got it, love!

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:41.245
Good work!

00:34:41.245 --> 00:34:43.414
One, two, and up!

00:34:46.250 --> 00:34:47.752
We got it, love!

00:34:47.752 --> 00:34:49.786
Ho!

00:34:49.786 --> 00:34:51.055
(starts chainsaw)

00:34:51.055 --> 00:34:52.256
All right!

00:34:52.256 --> 00:34:54.258
I'm gonna cut this block up in
half and see if we can move it.

00:34:54.258 --> 00:34:55.993
(revving chainsaw)

00:34:55.993 --> 00:35:02.900
(buzzing)

00:35:02.900 --> 00:35:04.101
JESSI: Nice!

00:35:04.101 --> 00:35:06.370
CHRIS: I'll go get the sled,
we'll pull the sled around,

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:10.241
and we'll try to work it
in there, huh?

00:35:10.241 --> 00:35:12.710
JESSI: This is the first year
that we've even harvested ice.

00:35:12.710 --> 00:35:14.444
You know, those chunks
in front of the house

00:35:14.444 --> 00:35:15.879
that we got out of the river.

00:35:15.879 --> 00:35:18.382
That was--I thought those
were big chunks, you know?

00:35:18.382 --> 00:35:19.683
But, dang.

00:35:19.683 --> 00:35:22.119
Uh, this is a bit bigger
of a chore

00:35:22.119 --> 00:35:24.321
than I was anticipating today.

00:35:24.321 --> 00:35:26.223
CHRIS: One, two, and up!

00:35:26.223 --> 00:35:27.225
JESSI: Holy crap!

00:35:27.225 --> 00:35:30.127
CHRIS: Come on!

00:35:30.127 --> 00:35:33.564
There's one.

00:35:33.564 --> 00:35:36.400
One, two, over!

00:35:36.400 --> 00:35:40.570
(grunting)

00:35:40.570 --> 00:35:41.606
JESSI: You're so strong, Chris.

00:35:41.606 --> 00:35:44.875
CHRIS: I got it.

00:35:44.875 --> 00:35:45.876
Okay.

00:35:45.876 --> 00:35:47.043
JESSI: Yeah!

00:35:47.043 --> 00:35:48.412
CHRIS: I'm just gonna go home,

00:35:48.412 --> 00:35:50.915
and I'll come back for you
if I can get it to move.

00:35:50.915 --> 00:35:54.951
You ready?

00:35:54.951 --> 00:35:57.888
JESSI: Go, go, go, go, go, go!

00:35:57.888 --> 00:36:00.724
If we do this again,
it's gotta be smaller pieces.

00:36:00.724 --> 00:36:03.127
That was like right on the cusp

00:36:03.127 --> 00:36:05.296
of just not being able
to do it at all

00:36:05.296 --> 00:36:06.864
under Chris and I's power.

00:36:06.864 --> 00:36:09.566
(snow machine roaring)

00:36:09.566 --> 00:36:12.002
♪ ♪

00:36:12.002 --> 00:36:14.371
ALEX: I think that any time
you take on a challenge

00:36:14.371 --> 00:36:18.242
and you see it through
to the end,

00:36:18.242 --> 00:36:21.211
it's gonna be, uh, you know,
a big positive growth.

00:36:21.211 --> 00:36:27.218
♪ ♪

00:36:27.218 --> 00:36:29.487
Hey, Sipsy.
Checking things out?

00:36:29.487 --> 00:36:30.788
I got the tree down.

00:36:30.788 --> 00:36:35.225
The next big problem I have is,
uh, the base of the tree.

00:36:35.225 --> 00:36:37.762
You know, I was hoping it
would have stayed right here,

00:36:37.762 --> 00:36:40.664
but it popped out, uh,
which, you know,

00:36:40.664 --> 00:36:41.932
could make it really susceptible

00:36:41.932 --> 00:36:44.268
to, uh, getting, you know,
washed away in the creek.

00:36:44.268 --> 00:36:46.037
'Cause this shore,
every time the water

00:36:46.037 --> 00:36:48.906
comes through each spring,
it recedes by a foot or so.

00:36:48.906 --> 00:36:51.976
So, if I can get it, you know,
up here a good ways,

00:36:51.976 --> 00:36:55.078
the bridge will last
for a while.

00:36:55.078 --> 00:36:57.348
So, the first thing I'm gonna do

00:36:57.348 --> 00:36:59.449
is put in some smaller logs
underneath

00:36:59.449 --> 00:37:00.951
to kind of act as skid logs

00:37:00.951 --> 00:37:04.555
to help guide the log
up to where I want it.

00:37:04.555 --> 00:37:06.656
So, you just give it a surface
that it's gonna glide over

00:37:06.656 --> 00:37:09.159
more easily than pulling it
on the ground.

00:37:09.159 --> 00:37:10.594
I got my skid log,
so that should help it

00:37:10.594 --> 00:37:12.162
just kind of glide
up into place.

00:37:12.162 --> 00:37:15.299
I'll go across and, uh, cut off
the excess part of the tree

00:37:15.299 --> 00:37:16.500
to make it lighter.

00:37:16.500 --> 00:37:18.836
That'll make it
a little easier to pull.

00:37:18.836 --> 00:37:19.970
Think I might cut it
right in here.

00:37:19.970 --> 00:37:21.071
It should be good.

00:37:21.071 --> 00:37:22.239
♪ ♪

00:37:22.239 --> 00:37:23.874
I'm not religious, but, um,

00:37:23.874 --> 00:37:26.776
I feel like there's a right way
for humans to live,

00:37:26.776 --> 00:37:28.512
and then there's a wrong way.

00:37:28.512 --> 00:37:31.648
Everything we already need
is already here, in nature.

00:37:31.648 --> 00:37:33.250
If you don't appreciate that,

00:37:33.250 --> 00:37:37.054
I think you're giving a big
middle finger to Mother Nature

00:37:37.054 --> 00:37:39.156
because, uh, no matter what,

00:37:39.156 --> 00:37:43.393
you live within and under
Mother Nature's rule.

00:37:43.393 --> 00:37:47.931
For humans to survive
indefinitely,

00:37:47.931 --> 00:37:49.399
we'll have to learn how to live

00:37:49.399 --> 00:37:53.204
by appreciating
what the Earth gives

00:37:53.204 --> 00:37:54.905
and not abusing it.

00:37:54.905 --> 00:37:57.207
That's good.

00:37:57.207 --> 00:37:59.142
(exhales)

00:37:59.142 --> 00:38:02.546
Let's, uh, give her a pull.

00:38:02.546 --> 00:38:05.216
If everything goes good,
it should just kind of glide

00:38:05.216 --> 00:38:07.918
right up these logs,
and that'd be it.

00:38:07.918 --> 00:38:09.486
Let's do it.

00:38:09.486 --> 00:38:12.589
(grunting)

00:38:12.589 --> 00:38:14.992
(bleep) thing's
not working right.

00:38:18.562 --> 00:38:21.532
That cheap piece of (bleep).

00:38:21.532 --> 00:38:22.533
Come on.

00:38:22.533 --> 00:38:24.268
Not a lot of things
get me frustrated,

00:38:24.268 --> 00:38:26.670
but one thing that gets me
frustrated pretty quick

00:38:26.670 --> 00:38:28.839
is, uh, cheap gear.

00:38:28.839 --> 00:38:32.276
When I get frustrated,
I tend to cuss and throw stuff.

00:38:32.276 --> 00:38:34.478
Thing's all (bleep) up.

00:38:34.478 --> 00:38:36.213
If I just kind of
let myself do that,

00:38:36.213 --> 00:38:38.048
then I don't carry
any frustration with me

00:38:38.048 --> 00:38:39.116
for the rest of the day,
you know,

00:38:39.116 --> 00:38:41.652
let my angry, inner primate
out when I need to.

00:38:41.652 --> 00:38:44.121
Keeps me from drinking.

00:38:44.121 --> 00:38:45.222
Oh!

00:38:45.222 --> 00:38:48.925
There she went!

00:38:48.925 --> 00:38:53.330
Pulling good now.

00:38:53.330 --> 00:38:55.165
Slow and steady.

00:38:55.165 --> 00:38:56.800
Just one notch at a time.

00:38:56.800 --> 00:39:01.705
♪ ♪

00:39:01.705 --> 00:39:03.940
Agh! (bleep)

00:39:03.940 --> 00:39:05.042
Piece of (bleep).

00:39:10.280 --> 00:39:13.883
ALEX: Agh! (bleep)
Piece of (bleep).

00:39:13.883 --> 00:39:14.985
I guess it got enough weight,

00:39:14.985 --> 00:39:18.055
it broke the, uh, skid logs
underneath it.

00:39:18.055 --> 00:39:19.790
It's on the shore,
but just barely.

00:39:19.790 --> 00:39:21.291
That digging into the ground

00:39:21.291 --> 00:39:23.060
is going to make it
really hard to pull.

00:39:23.060 --> 00:39:25.896
Probably I can, uh, pull 'em
from the middle,

00:39:25.896 --> 00:39:27.163
somewhere in here.

00:39:27.163 --> 00:39:29.567
If I can scoot it over
just a couple feet, uh,

00:39:29.567 --> 00:39:31.234
laterally, you know,
to the side.

00:39:31.234 --> 00:39:33.938
That should allow the end
of the log to travel free

00:39:33.938 --> 00:39:38.141
without digging into the snow
and getting stuck.

00:39:38.141 --> 00:39:40.877
This tree, because it's so
massive and it weighs so much,

00:39:40.877 --> 00:39:43.347
my options for moving it
are really limited.

00:39:43.347 --> 00:39:46.717
With this two-ton pulley, I'm
able to slowly, inch by inch,

00:39:46.717 --> 00:39:48.485
kind of drag it
a little bit this way.

00:39:48.485 --> 00:39:49.620
And that's about it.

00:39:49.620 --> 00:39:51.888
When you're working alone
on a project like this,

00:39:51.888 --> 00:39:54.258
you'll either become patient
or you'll have to quit.

00:39:54.258 --> 00:39:55.258
As they say in the Army,

00:39:55.258 --> 00:39:57.160
"Slow is smooth
and smooth is fast."

00:39:57.160 --> 00:40:01.164
♪ ♪

00:40:01.164 --> 00:40:02.899
Okay.

00:40:02.899 --> 00:40:04.468
Well, that's that.

00:40:04.468 --> 00:40:06.303
I felt a relief
to have this done.

00:40:06.303 --> 00:40:07.671
That was pretty difficult.

00:40:07.671 --> 00:40:09.773
I mean, you could see moving
this much weight, you know,

00:40:09.773 --> 00:40:11.275
five feet, ten feet,

00:40:11.275 --> 00:40:15.078
I mean, it's taken,
like, a lot of work.

00:40:15.078 --> 00:40:17.581
So, now that I've got the tree
where I want it,

00:40:17.581 --> 00:40:20.116
I'm gonna collect whatever
scrap lumber I got

00:40:20.116 --> 00:40:23.720
and just basically piece
together a flat walking surface.

00:40:23.720 --> 00:40:27.925
♪ ♪

00:40:27.925 --> 00:40:31.428
Whenever I do something,
I tend to go into it 110%.

00:40:31.428 --> 00:40:33.297
I'm either doing it
all the way or not.

00:40:33.297 --> 00:40:36.033
I guess at the end of the day,
we all just want to feel good.

00:40:36.033 --> 00:40:39.870
The satisfaction I get from
taking on a tough challenge,

00:40:39.870 --> 00:40:42.739
working hard,
facing the natural challenges

00:40:42.739 --> 00:40:45.442
Mother Nature gives you
here in Alaska,

00:40:45.442 --> 00:40:47.043
I find that to be
more satisfying

00:40:47.043 --> 00:40:51.548
than living an easy,
decadent life in the city.

00:40:51.548 --> 00:40:52.749
I approve of this bridge.

00:40:52.749 --> 00:40:53.751
It's gonna work for me,

00:40:53.751 --> 00:40:55.718
but, uh, I think I gotta
get the inspector

00:40:55.718 --> 00:40:58.588
to get the, uh, final approval.

00:40:58.588 --> 00:41:00.557
Atta boy, there you go.

00:41:00.557 --> 00:41:02.392
Aw, Sipsy, I'm so proud of you.

00:41:02.392 --> 00:41:04.394
Bridge is done.
I'm pretty happy with it.

00:41:04.394 --> 00:41:05.763
In case of an emergency,

00:41:05.763 --> 00:41:09.299
I'll still be able
to get across the creek.

00:41:09.299 --> 00:41:11.001
Sipsy gave his seal of approval.

00:41:11.001 --> 00:41:12.569
This spring, when the creek
is running,

00:41:12.569 --> 00:41:15.039
Sipsy will, uh, happily run
back and forth across

00:41:15.039 --> 00:41:17.574
so he can chase the squirrels
wherever they go.

00:41:17.574 --> 00:41:22.413
 ♪ Fed to the wolves
 without retreat ♪

00:41:22.413 --> 00:41:25.215
 ♪ Mm mm mm mmm ♪

00:41:25.215 --> 00:41:26.550
JESSI: Can you get me
just a little bit more,

00:41:26.550 --> 00:41:27.918
uh, hot water, please?

00:41:27.918 --> 00:41:29.820
CHRIS: Yeah.
Give me just a second.

00:41:29.820 --> 00:41:31.955
♪ ♪

00:41:31.955 --> 00:41:34.724
JESSI: We're lucky if we get
a bath once a week out here.

00:41:34.724 --> 00:41:36.861
It just takes a lot of water.

00:41:39.630 --> 00:41:41.565
I don't know if it's any harder
out here this year.

00:41:41.565 --> 00:41:44.434
I just, I am more willing?

00:41:44.434 --> 00:41:46.503
I'm just pushing myself more.

00:41:46.503 --> 00:41:48.906
As long as you have
a good, optimistic, you know,

00:41:48.906 --> 00:41:51.508
feeling in your body, and you're
just happy to be here,

00:41:51.508 --> 00:41:53.077
no matter how hard
the work gets,

00:41:53.077 --> 00:41:54.478
you're gonna get it done.

00:41:54.478 --> 00:41:56.880
 ♪ You got the devil
 inside you ♪

00:41:56.880 --> 00:41:59.516
 ♪ You set the church
 on fire ♪

00:41:59.516 --> 00:42:05.789
♪ ♪

00:42:05.789 --> 00:42:07.224
BRENT: There she is!
You made it!

00:42:07.224 --> 00:42:10.160
IDA: I made it, yeah,
it's nice to be back.

00:42:10.160 --> 00:42:11.962
Wow, look at that roof!

00:42:11.962 --> 00:42:15.198
BRENT: Yep, got some snow off,
got all the glaciers off.

00:42:15.198 --> 00:42:16.767
I'm sure you're cold
from your trip.

00:42:16.767 --> 00:42:18.568
We should head inside
with some wood.

00:42:18.568 --> 00:42:20.703
IDA: Home sweet home.

00:42:20.703 --> 00:42:24.708
 ♪ Day of rust
 and nights of rain ♪

00:42:24.708 --> 00:42:27.344
I can tell that you've been busy
with all of these little things

00:42:27.344 --> 00:42:29.179
that we've had on the list
for a while.

00:42:29.179 --> 00:42:30.180
BRENT: Yeah, yeah, it's been

00:42:30.180 --> 00:42:31.748
a really productive
couple of days.

00:42:31.748 --> 00:42:34.718
So, we'll just keep
whittling away at it.

00:42:34.718 --> 00:42:37.187
Three chores down,
six more added.

00:42:37.187 --> 00:42:39.623
IDA: Yeah.
That's part of the life.

00:42:39.623 --> 00:42:40.991
BRENT: That's the nature
of the game,

00:42:40.991 --> 00:42:43.127
but for now,
let's enjoy our fire

00:42:43.127 --> 00:42:46.096
and get a good night's sleep
and start it up tomorrow.

00:42:46.096 --> 00:42:48.431
IDA: Yeah, it's really good
to be back home.

00:42:48.431 --> 00:42:51.201
BRENT: He's happy you're back.

00:42:51.201 --> 00:42:53.270
IDA: I always have time
for the pets.

00:42:53.270 --> 00:42:56.640
 ♪ You got, you got the devil
 inside you ♪

00:42:56.640 --> 00:43:00.277
 ♪ You set the church on fire ♪

00:43:00.277 --> 00:43:03.547
 ♪ You got, you got the devil
 inside you ♪

00:43:03.547 --> 00:43:04.814
MICHAEL: Dogs are all fed,

00:43:04.814 --> 00:43:07.450
settled in their houses,
sleeping like babies.

00:43:07.450 --> 00:43:10.154
I'm gonna go ahead
and enjoy myself a nice meal

00:43:10.154 --> 00:43:11.921
after a hard training run.

00:43:11.921 --> 00:43:13.690
You know, the focus of that trip

00:43:13.690 --> 00:43:15.926
was to train the dogs
in a new environment.

00:43:15.926 --> 00:43:18.261
Now that they've done
back-to-back five-mile runs

00:43:18.261 --> 00:43:21.465
with a camping trip, it's time
to increase that mileage.

00:43:21.465 --> 00:43:22.799
These dogs are
getting to the point

00:43:22.799 --> 00:43:24.501
where it's time to get
on the trail

00:43:24.501 --> 00:43:28.171
and go on an expedition
or hunt caribou.

00:43:28.171 --> 00:43:31.041
So here we got a nice
pan-fried rainbow trout

00:43:31.041 --> 00:43:32.976
right on the wood stove.

00:43:32.976 --> 00:43:35.311
This is, uh, just
icing on the cake

00:43:35.311 --> 00:43:39.015
after a fun run with the dogs.

00:43:39.015 --> 00:43:40.817
This is my way of life.

00:43:40.817 --> 00:43:45.055
You know, these dogs are
an extension of my vision

00:43:45.055 --> 00:43:47.791
of having the most authentic
Alaskan lifestyle

00:43:47.791 --> 00:43:51.061
that I can live.

00:43:51.061 --> 00:43:54.297
They're my athletes, they're
my hunting and fishing buddies.

00:43:54.297 --> 00:43:58.034
And I could never picture living
in Alaska without sled dogs.

00:43:58.034 --> 00:44:00.270
You know, if I ever got rid
of these dogs,

00:44:00.270 --> 00:44:02.840
there'd probably be
an empty void in my heart.

00:44:02.840 --> 00:44:06.810
Together as a team, we'll
continue on and do great things.

00:44:06.810 --> 00:44:09.246
 ♪ You got the devil
 inside you ♪

00:44:09.246 --> 00:44:10.514
 ♪ The devil inside you ♪

